ustment": 0.23, "key_factors": ["Recent polls show Hamilton narrowly ahead despite Kansas's Republican lean", "Hamilton's large fundraising advantage over incumbent Marshall", "Expert ratings overwhelmingly call the race a Tossup", "Prediction markets price the race near even, slightly favoring Hamilton"], "rationale": "Late-October polls from Emerson, WaPo/SSPG and SSRS show Hamilton up 3 to 9 points, and averages put him ahead by roughly 3, offsetting Kansas's Republican baseline. Hamilton has also outraised Marshall by a wide margin, while markets and most raters see a coin-flip race with a slight Democratic edge.", "watch": "A late shift among rural and farm-belt voters back toward Marshall, or consolidation of Republican-leaning undecideds, could flip this tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Roger Marshall (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Kansas's Senate race has become one of the cycle's most competitive, with Democrat Adam Hamilton running even with or slightly ahead of incumbent Roger Marshall in recent polling. Surveys from Emerson, The Washington Post/SSPG and SSRS in late October all show Hamilton leading, and polling averages put him up by roughly three points in a state that normally favors Republicans by eight.\n\nHamilton has raised far more money than Marshall, and outside spending has made this the most expensive election in Kansas history. Most expert raters call the race a Tossup, and prediction markets price Hamilton's chances just above even. The race remains genuinely uncertain, with rural voter sentiment and late turnout decisions likely to decide the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2893, "margin": -2.76}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3585, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4222, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4301, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3024, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2988, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026